          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "How many ways can the letters of the word ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "PERMUTATION",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " be arranged if",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "there are no restrictions.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$19\\,958\\,400$",
          "worked": "There are 11 letters, including two identical T’s.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\frac{11!}{2!}\\\\N&=19\\,958\\,400.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "the T’s are not together.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$16\\,329\\,600$\n\nSubtract arrangements with the two T’s in one block.",
          "worked": "Treat TT as one element. The block and the other nine letters give $10!$ arrangements with the T’s together.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\frac{11!}{2!}-10!\\\\N&=19\\,958\\,400-3\\,628\\,800\\\\N&=16\\,329\\,600.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q12-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "the vowels appear in alphabetical order, though not necessarily together.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$166\\,320$\n\nOnly one of the $5!$ orders of the five distinct vowels is alphabetical.",
          "worked": "The vowels are A, E, I, O and U. For each choice of vowel positions, only one of their $5!$ orders is alphabetical.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\frac{11!}{2!}\\div5!\\\\N&=\\frac{11!}{2!\\times5!}\\\\N&=166\\,320.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
